# std::alloc_policy — allocator policy as a first-class library contract
# (audit item P1-12)
#
# The audit contract: hot paths need EXPLICIT guarantees about where the
# objects they touch come from — a slab allocator for the fixed-size
# object pools, a bump arena per market-data packet/batch, a
# preallocated order pool, a fixed-capacity ring, NO allocator in the
# matching loop (finance), and (embedded) a static arena + ArrayVec +
# fixed ring with no heap at all. Memory safety does not require a
# universal general-purpose allocator — it requires that every hot loop
# CHOOSES a policy and that the choice is a first-class, documented
# fact of the module that owns the loop.
#
# This module makes the policy a first-class library contract:
#   - AllocPolicy — the policy MARKERS (enum): Heap / Arena / Slab /
#     Fixed / None, with the audit's mapping spelled out on the enum;
#   - BumpArena — the deterministic bump (arena) allocator: owned
#     (new / new_aligned) or borrowed (with_buffer /
#     with_buffer_aligned) backing, alignment-honoring alloc /
#     alloc_layout / reset, no individual free, zero-overhead reset;
#   - SlabPool[T] — the fixed-size typed object pool: preallocated
#     slots, acquire/release index hand-off over an intrusive free
#     list, no heap churn after init, exact drop of live occupants
#     with deterministic ascending teardown;
#   - ArenaVec[T] — the arena-backed fixed builder (the audit's
#     allocator-generic-collections row): a Vec-like push/get/len/
#     reset surface over a BumpArena it owns; T: Copy; capacity fixed
#     at construction; ZERO general-heap traffic after init.
#
# WHERE THE POLICIES LIVE IN THE KERNEL STD (the call surface this
# module documents and marks):
#   - std::alloc (kernel family) is the low-level allocator authority:
#     trait Allocator, SystemAllocator (the runtime memory authority
#     behind _tg_mem_alloc/_tg_mem_free), ArenaAllocator/ArenaBuf
#     (policy: Arena), PoolAllocator (policy: Slab), the layout API and
#     the global alloc functions. Its alignment contract (any
#     power-of-two align <= 16; over-aligned requests are explicit
#     errors) is the BACKING contract of this module: the kernel
#     runtime payload is 16-aligned and cannot do better itself. The
#     policy forms BELOW therefore carry the extra alignment in THEIR
#     OWN backings — a BumpArena (new_aligned) or SlabPool slot base is
#     pre-aligned up to the 64-byte anchor (the widest target vector
#     alignment) at construction, so their alloc surface serves every
#     power-of-two align through 64; the zero-headroom default
#     (BumpArena::new, with_buffer) keeps the std::alloc <= 16
#     contract (see the ALIGNMENT CONTRACT notes in the BumpArena/
#     SlabPool sections).
#   - std::collections::Vec growth is HEAP by construction: push lowers
#     to the compiler intrinsic __intrinsic_array_push (the runtime
#     allocator), so a general allocator parameter on Vec[T, A] is
#     kernel type-system/codegen work — documented future work below.
#   - std::fixed (native family) delivers the policy Fixed/None forms:
#     ArrayVecN / RingBufferN / SpscQueueN with inline [Option[T]; N]
#     storage, no allocator, no locks.
#   - This module (std::alloc_policy) adds the policy markers and the
#     deterministic policy forms (BumpArena, SlabPool[T]) that the
#     audit's hot paths call directly.
#
# DROP DISCIPLINE + THE NON-COPY PROPERTY (deallocate exactly once):
# an owner of MEMORY holds a raw pointer and declares the finalizer —
# the std::alloc Box/Arc and std::fixed SpscQueue split-endpoint record
# pattern. Both BumpArena and SlabPool are plain structs (raw Ptr +
# UInt fields) with a registered `impl Drop`: the Drop releases exactly
# the init-time allocations once, then nulls every field so that an
# EARLY or MANUAL release path is idempotent.
#
# IMPORTANT (audit correction): field nulling is NOT the double-free
# defense. A bit-copied pair of an owning struct holds INDEPENDENT
# field storage — nulling copy A's fields never reaches copy B, and B
# still releases the shared backing, so a bit-copied owner double-frees
# no matter how thoroughly Drop nulls its own fields. The REAL safety
# property is the compiler's TYPE-PROPERTY ENGINE: a registered
# `impl Drop` excludes a type from the structural bit-copy set (the
# same mechanism that makes Vec/Box/Rc/String non-Copy —
# tg_compiler/types.tg is_named_trivially_copyable: "a registered
# Drop requires destruction: a raw bit-copy would duplicate the state
# the drop glue releases"), so BumpArena and SlabPool[T] (and every
# type embedding them, e.g. ArenaVec) are classified non-Copy and a
# bit-copy of them is a COMPILE ERROR — ownership transfers by
# move/sink only and Drop runs exactly once on the single live value.
# The classification is pinned by the module's compile-fail canaries
# (tests/canary_neg — the unit lane's negative convention for other
# std types: SlabPool[String].get is rejected the same way
# RingBuffer[String].peek_front is). The nulling itself stays as
# defense-in-depth: Drop remains idempotent under any manual teardown
# interleaving.
# A struct that owns VALUES holds them structurally and declares no
# finalizer — here the SlabPool occupant VALUES are owned by raw
# memory, so their destruction is the pool's own Drop/clear duty (each
# live occupant destructed exactly once, in ascending slot order).
#
# THREAD SAFETY: BumpArena and SlabPool are single-owner, NOT
# thread-safe. Nothing in this module locks; the per-pool free list and
# the bump pointer are plain fields of one owning value. (The runtime
# allocator's own locks behind global_alloc/global_dealloc apply only
# at init/teardown.)
#
# Module identity: std::alloc_policy (the file-based module — same
# convention as std/collections.tg and std/fixed.tg: no `module` block;
# the file name is the module path).

use std::core::{Option, Result, Unit, Bool, UInt, Int, panic}
use std::alloc::{global_alloc, global_dealloc, size_of, align_of, Layout}

# [METADATA]
# module: tg::alloc_policy
# status: alpha
# effects: []
# capabilities: []


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# AllocPolicy — the policy markers (the P1-12 audit contract in one type)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The audit contract, in one type: every hot path chooses ONE allocation
# policy up front, and the marker makes the choice a first-class,
# documented fact. The mapping the audit item names:
#
#   Heap  — general-purpose allocation. The std::alloc authority
#           (SystemAllocator / the global alloc / the runtime behind
#           String/Vec/Map growth) and today's Vec growth path (the
#           __intrinsic_array_push intrinsic). The default when no
#           fixed bound exists; never the choice INSIDE a bounded hot
#           loop.
#   Arena — the bump arena: BumpArena (this module) and std::alloc's
#           ArenaAllocator/ArenaBuf. Per market-data packet / per
#           batch: deterministic bump pointer, free-all at reset or
#           teardown, no individual free.
#   Slab  — the fixed-size object pool: SlabPool[T] (this module) and
#           std::alloc's PoolAllocator. Preallocated slots for
#           per-symbol / per-order state; acquire/release index
#           hand-off; no heap churn after init.
#   Fixed — the inline fixed-capacity containers of std::fixed
#           (ArrayVecN / RingBufferN / SpscQueueN): the capacity is
#           part of the type name, the storage is inline [Option[T]; N],
#           and there is no allocation after construction.
#   None  — no allocator in the path at all: fixed.tg containers,
#           BumpArena::with_buffer over a static buffer (embedded), and
#           the finance matching loop, which touches only preallocated
#           pools and rings and never calls an allocator.
#
# The audit's hot-path mapping (the sentence to quote in code review):
#   matching loop (finance)     -> None (or Fixed for bounded staging);
#                                  no allocator call exists in the loop
#   market-data packet / batch  -> Arena (BumpArena; free-all at the
#                                  packet/batch boundary)
#   per-symbol state / orders   -> Slab (SlabPool[T] / PoolAllocator:
#                                  preallocated, acquire/release only)
#   bounded queues              -> Fixed (the fixed-capacity rings)
#   embedded                    -> None + Fixed + with_buffer (static
#                                  arena, ArrayVec, fixed ring — no heap
#                                  at all after static init)
#   Vec growth                  -> Heap today (intrinsic growth); the
#                                  arena-provided builder and the
#                                  Vec[T, A] / Map[K, V, H, A] forms are
#                                  documented future work (module header)
enum AllocPolicy
  Heap
  Arena
  Slab
  Fixed
  None
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE ALIGNMENT MODEL (the audit's Layout { size, align } contract)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Anchor: the supported maximum alignment of this module's policy
# allocators is 64 bytes — the widest vector width the target
# layout/ABI engine emits (the 64-byte SIMD forms of the vector
# row). Every power-of-two align <= 64 is honored; an align above the
# anchor is an EXPLICIT error, never a silently misaligned buffer.
#
# The kernel runtime allocator (std::alloc's SystemAllocator /
# global_alloc) guarantees a 16-aligned payload and rejects >16
# itself — so a backing obtained from it cannot serve 32/64-aligned
# sub-allocations by offset math alone. The policy allocators carry
# the extra alignment in THEIR OWN BACKING: the construction request
# is padded by at most (max_align - 16) bytes and the base is aligned
# up to max_align before the first alloc. The shift is fixed per
# backing (deterministic: reset-then-realloc returns the SAME
# addresses), the padded request is released with the raw base and
# the exact request size (the runtime free reads the block header, so
# the size argument is ABI-only), and the served capacity is the
# requested capacity exactly.
#
# Borrowed backings (BumpArena::with_buffer / with_buffer_aligned)
# cannot be pre-aligned by the arena; the caller must supply a buffer
# aligned to every align it will request, and alloc() VERIFIES each
# returned address — a misaligned borrowed base for the requested align
# is an explicit error at the alloc site.
def policy_max_align() -> UInt
  64
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BumpArena — deterministic bump (arena) allocator
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The policy form of std::alloc's ArenaAllocator: identical bump math,
# plus the borrowed-buffer form (with_buffer) that makes the arena
# usable over a static/owned buffer with NO allocation of its own
# (embedded policy). BumpArena is NOT thread-safe. reset() is a single
# store — zero-overhead free-all; it never touches the buffer contents
# (no memset), so bump determinism across resets is exact: after a
# reset the same alloc sequence returns the same addresses.
#
# ALIGNMENT CONTRACT (Layout { size, align }-honoring): alloc(size,
# align) serves every power-of-two align through the arena's
# configured max (policy_max_align() = 64 — the widest target vector
# alignment). The OWNED backing (new / new_aligned) comes from the
# global allocator, whose payload is only 16-aligned; the arena
# therefore pre-aligns its own base: the construction request carries
# up to (max_align - 16) headroom bytes and the base is aligned up to
# max_align at new() — new(capacity) arenas serve aligns <= 16 with no
# headroom (the std::alloc contract, zero overhead), new_aligned(cap,
# align) arenas serve through their declared max (the audit's ≥ 64
# row). An align 0, a non-power-of-two, or an align above the arena's
# configured max is an EXPLICIT error (panic) that names the fix.
# For with_buffer the CALLER supplies the backing and must provide a
# buffer aligned to every align it will request; the arena's contract
# for the plain borrowed form is aligns <= 16 (with_buffer_aligned
# raises the claim for caller-pre-aligned backings). alloc VERIFIES
# each returned address and panics on a misaligned borrowed base (the
# arena cannot re-align a borrowed buffer). alloc_layout(layout) is
# the Layout entry point (std::alloc::Layout { size, align }).
#
# Exhaustion: alloc returns a null pointer (never a partial bump); the
# caller observes it and resets or extends the arena — allocation
# failures inside a batch are a policy decision, not a panic. A
# zero-size alloc returns null without advancing the bump pointer
# (the std::alloc zero-size contract).

struct BumpArena
  _buffer: Ptr[u8]    # the aligned bump base — the first byte alloc hands out
  _alloc: Ptr[u8]     # the raw backing allocation base (== _buffer when borrowed)
  _capacity: UInt     # served bytes (the requested capacity, exactly)
  _raw_bytes: UInt    # owned: the exact global-allocator request (capacity + headroom)
  _offset: UInt       # the bump pointer (next allocation offset)
  _owns: UInt         # 1 = owned backing (Drop releases); 0 = borrowed
  _max_align: UInt    # this arena's supported-max alignment (16 or a power of two <= 64)
end

impl BumpArena
  # Owned arena, the std::alloc alignment contract (power-of-two
  # aligns <= 16): the backing is allocated ONCE from the global
  # allocator (16-aligned payload, NO headroom) and released exactly
  # once at Drop. Allocation happens only here — every later operation
  # (alloc/reset) is bump-pointer work with no allocator involvement.
  def new(capacity: UInt) -> BumpArena
    BumpArena::new_aligned(capacity, 16)
  end

  # Owned arena pre-aligned to `max_align` (a power of two in
  # [16, policy_max_align()]): alloc() honors every power-of-two align
  # through `max_align`. The construction request is padded by up to
  # (max_align - 16) bytes so the base can be aligned up; the served
  # capacity is `capacity` exactly and the release uses the raw base
  # and the padded request.
  def new_aligned(capacity: UInt, max_align: UInt) -> BumpArena
    if max_align == 0 || (max_align & (max_align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("BumpArena::new_aligned: max_align must be a power of two")
    end
    if max_align < 16 then
      panic("BumpArena::new_aligned: max_align below 16 is not supported (16 is the backing's guaranteed alignment)")
    end
    if max_align > policy_max_align() then
      panic("BumpArena::new_aligned: max_align " + max_align.to_string() + " > " + policy_max_align().to_string() + " not supported (the alignment anchor is the target's widest 64-byte vector alignment)")
    end
    let request = if capacity == 0 then 0 else capacity + (max_align - 16) end
    let raw = global_alloc(request, 16)
    if request != 0 && raw.is_null() then
      panic("BumpArena::new_aligned: allocation failed - out of memory")
    end
    # Align the base up to max_align (the raw payload is 16-aligned,
    # so the shift is a multiple of 16 and at most max_align - 16).
    mut aligned = raw
    if request != 0 then
      let mis = (raw as Int) & ((max_align as Int) - 1)
      if mis != 0 then
        aligned = (raw as Int + ((max_align as Int) - mis)) as Ptr[u8]
      end
    end
    BumpArena {
      _buffer: aligned,
      _alloc: raw,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _raw_bytes: request,
      _offset: 0,
      _owns: 1,
      _max_align: max_align
    }
  end

  # Borrowed arena: bumps over a CALLER-OWNED buffer (static/global
  # memory for the embedded policy, a preallocated region, etc.). The
  # arena never frees it — Drop is a no-op on the backing — and the
  # caller must keep the buffer alive (and aligned to every align it
  # will request, 16-byte multiples for the <= 16 contract) for the
  # arena's whole lifetime. alloc() verifies each returned address.
  def with_buffer(buffer: Ptr[u8], capacity: UInt) -> BumpArena
    BumpArena {
      _buffer: buffer,
      _alloc: buffer,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _raw_bytes: 0,
      _offset: 0,
      _owns: 0,
      _max_align: 16
    }
  end

  # Borrowed arena over a backing the CALLER pre-aligned to `max_align`
  # (a power of two in [16, policy_max_align()] — e.g. a static buffer
  # placed at an over-aligned address for the embedded policy). The
  # arena trusts the caller's claim (it cannot re-align a borrowed
  # buffer) and alloc() VERIFIES every returned address — a base that
  # cannot honor the requested align is an explicit error at the alloc
  # site, never a silently misaligned buffer.
  def with_buffer_aligned(buffer: Ptr[u8], capacity: UInt, max_align: UInt) -> BumpArena
    if max_align == 0 || (max_align & (max_align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("BumpArena::with_buffer_aligned: max_align must be a power of two")
    end
    if max_align < 16 then
      panic("BumpArena::with_buffer_aligned: max_align below 16 is not supported (16 is the backing's guaranteed alignment)")
    end
    if max_align > policy_max_align() then
      panic("BumpArena::with_buffer_aligned: max_align " + max_align.to_string() + " > " + policy_max_align().to_string() + " not supported (the alignment anchor is the target's widest 64-byte vector alignment)")
    end
    BumpArena {
      _buffer: buffer,
      _alloc: buffer,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _raw_bytes: 0,
      _offset: 0,
      _owns: 0,
      _max_align: max_align
    }
  end

  def capacity(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity
  end

  # Bytes bumped so far (the deterministic bump pointer position).
  def used(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._offset
  end

  def remaining(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity - self._offset
  end

  # The arena's configured supported-max alignment.
  def max_align(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._max_align
  end

  # The aligned backing base (owned or borrowed) — the first byte
  # alloc hands out. Read-only access to the arena's memory for the
  # caller that needs to hand the batch out; writes to it must respect
  # the bump discipline (bytes above _offset belong to the next alloc).
  def buffer(self: Self) -> Ptr[u8]
    self._buffer
  end

  # Allocate `size` bytes aligned to `align` (a power of two <= the
  # arena's configured max). Returns null on exhaustion; panics on an
  # out-of-contract align or on a borrowed base that cannot honor it.
  # No individual free exists — the whole arena is reclaimed by
  # reset() or by Drop.
  def alloc(inout self: Self, size: UInt, align: UInt) -> Ptr[u8]
    if align == 0 || (align & (align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("BumpArena::alloc: align must be a power of two")
    end
    if align > self._max_align then
      panic("BumpArena::alloc: align " + align.to_string() + " > this arena's max " + self._max_align.to_string() + " not supported (new() arenas serve aligns <= 16; construct with BumpArena::new_aligned(capacity, max_align) for aligns through " + policy_max_align().to_string() + ")")
    end
    if size == 0 then
      return Ptr::null()
    end
    if self._buffer.is_null() || self._capacity == 0 then
      return Ptr::null()
    end

    # Align the current bump offset (the same aligned-offset math as
    # std::alloc's ArenaAllocator, with the same overflow guards).
    let align_mask = align - 1
    let aligned_offset = (self._offset + align_mask) & ~align_mask

    if aligned_offset < self._offset then
      return Ptr::null()
    end

    let end_offset = aligned_offset + size
    if end_offset < aligned_offset then
      return Ptr::null()
    end

    if end_offset > self._capacity then
      return Ptr::null()  # exhausted: the caller resets or extends
    end

    let ptr = self._buffer.offset(aligned_offset as Int)
    # Verify the returned address (only a borrowed base that the caller
    # failed to align for this request can trip this — the owned bases
    # are pre-aligned to the arena's max at construction).
    if ((ptr as Int) & ((align as Int) - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("BumpArena::alloc: the backing buffer is not aligned for align " + align.to_string() + " (with_buffer: supply a buffer aligned to every align you request — " + self._max_align.to_string() + " for the full contract)")
    end
    self._offset = end_offset
    ptr
  end

  # The Layout-honoring entry point (the audit's Layout { size, align }
  # surface): serves the layout's size at the layout's align under the
  # arena's contract — align must be a power of two within the arena's
  # configured max. Bump allocations are not padded to the layout's
  # stride; the requested size is served exactly.
  def alloc_layout(inout self: Self, layout: Layout) -> Ptr[u8]
    self.alloc(layout.size as UInt, layout.align as UInt)
  end

  # Zero-overhead free-all: ONE store to the bump pointer. The buffer
  # contents are untouched, so a reset-then-realloc cycle returns the
  # SAME addresses for the same sequence (deterministic bump —
  # alignment included: the base shift is fixed at construction).
  def reset(inout self: Self) -> Unit
    self._offset = 0
  end
end

impl Drop for BumpArena
  def drop(inout self: BumpArena) -> Unit
    # Release the backing exactly once (owned arenas only — the raw
    # allocation base and its exact padded request). Field nulling
    # keeps an early/manual release idempotent; the double-free
    # defense is the type engine's non-Copy classification (a
    # bit-copied second owner cannot be formed — see the DROP
    # DISCIPLINE note in the module header).
    if self._owns != 0 && !self._alloc.is_null() && self._raw_bytes != 0 then
      global_dealloc(self._alloc, self._raw_bytes, 16)
    end
    self._buffer = Ptr::null()
    self._alloc = Ptr::null()
    self._capacity = 0
    self._raw_bytes = 0
    self._offset = 0
    self._owns = 0
    self._max_align = 0
  end
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SlabPool[T] — fixed-size typed object pool (the slab policy)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Preallocated slots for the per-symbol / per-order hot path: init does
# the pool's allocations (the slot arena + the free-list/occupancy
# metadata); acquire/release/clear afterwards are pure free-list work —
# NO heap churn after init. SlabPool is NOT thread-safe.
#
# INDEX HAND-OFF: acquire(inout, sink item) consumes the item into a
# free slot and returns the slot INDEX; release(inout, index) moves the
# occupant back out (ownership transfers to the caller) and returns it.
# The index is an opaque handle into the pool — the language has no
# safe reference types, so value-in/value-out index hand-off is the
# sound surface (never a raw pointer into the slab). release on an
# out-of-range or already-vacant index returns None (a double release
# is a no-op, never a corruption).
#
# SLOT MEMORY + EXACT DROP: the slot arena is raw bytes; a slot holds a
# live T exactly while its occupancy flag is 1. acquire writes the item
# first and sets the flag LAST (the flag is the drop authority);
# release clears the flag FIRST after moving the value out. Teardown —
# both clear() and the registered Drop — walks the slots in ASCENDING
# index order and destructs every live occupant exactly once (clear
# through release-and-discard, Drop through drop_in_place on the live
# slots), then clear leaves a fully reusable empty pool and Drop
# releases the init-time allocations.
#
# The free list is intrusive over the metadata buffer: _free_next[i] is
# the next free slot after i, with the sentinel END = _capacity; the
# list is LIFO (release pushes onto the head), so a freshly released
# slot is the next one acquire hands out — deterministic reuse.
#
# ALIGNMENT CONTRACT (typed slots keep their element alignment): the
# slot arena comes from the global allocator (16-aligned payload) and
# the pool pre-aligns its slot base up to the ELEMENT's alignment at
# new() (headroom request, exactly like BumpArena::new_aligned), so
# every power-of-two element alignment through the anchor
# (policy_max_align() = 64) is served; an element alignment above the
# anchor is an EXPLICIT error at new() (the pool cannot honor
# over-aligned slots). Slot i lives at base + i * stride, where the
# stride is size_of[T] ROUNDED UP to the element alignment — the ABI
# does not guarantee size % align == 0 for every type (std::alloc's
# layout_array rounds array strides for the same reason), and an
# unrounded stride would misalign every slot after slot 0. The
# metadata buffers (free-list + occupancy words) are 16-aligned.

struct SlabPool[T]
  _slots: Ptr[u8]       # the aligned element arena base (capacity * stride bytes)
  _slots_alloc: Ptr[u8] # the raw slot-arena allocation base (the release target)
  _stride: UInt         # element stride: size_of[T] rounded up to align_of[T]
  _slots_bytes: UInt   # the exact slot-arena request (stride * capacity + headroom)
  _free_next: Ptr[u8]  # free-list metadata: capacity UInt words (next[i])
  _live: Ptr[u8]       # occupancy metadata: capacity UInt words (0/1)
  _meta_bytes: UInt    # capacity * word — the exact release size
  _capacity: UInt      # slot count; also the free-list END sentinel
  _free_head: UInt     # free-list head; == _capacity when the pool is full
  _len: UInt           # live occupant count
end

impl[T] SlabPool[T]
  # Creates a pool of `capacity` preallocated slots. All pool
  # allocations happen here (slot arena + two metadata buffers, each
  # through the global allocator) and are released exactly once by
  # Drop. A zero capacity creates an empty pool that rejects every
  # acquire.
  def new(capacity: UInt) -> SlabPool[T]
    if capacity == 0 then
      return SlabPool {
        _slots: Ptr::null(),
        _slots_alloc: Ptr::null(),
        _stride: 0,
        _slots_bytes: 0,
        _free_next: Ptr::null(),
        _live: Ptr::null(),
        _meta_bytes: 0,
        _capacity: 0,
        _free_head: 0,
        _len: 0
      }
    end
    let elem_size = size_of[T]()
    if elem_size == 0 then
      panic("SlabPool::new: zero-size element types are not supported")
    end
    let elem_align = align_of[T]()
    if elem_align == 0 || (elem_align & (elem_align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("SlabPool::new: element alignment must be a power of two")
    end
    if elem_align > policy_max_align() then
      panic("SlabPool::new: element alignment " + elem_align.to_string() + " > " + policy_max_align().to_string() + " not supported - slots are aligned through the 64-byte anchor (the widest vector alignment)")
    end
    # The element stride: size_of[T] rounded up to the element
    # alignment, so EVERY slot address (not just slot 0) is aligned.
    let stride_mask = elem_align - 1
    let elem_stride = (elem_size + stride_mask) & ~stride_mask
    if elem_stride < elem_size then
      panic("SlabPool::new: element stride overflow")
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]()
    let slots_bytes = elem_stride * capacity
    if slots_bytes / capacity != elem_stride then
      panic("SlabPool::new: slot arena overflow")
    end
    let meta_bytes = word * capacity
    if meta_bytes / capacity != word then
      panic("SlabPool::new: metadata overflow")
    end
    # Slot-arena request with element-alignment headroom (a 16-aligned
    # payload needs no shift for element aligns <= 16; aligning up to
    # a 32/64 element alignment costs at most elem_align - 16 bytes).
    let headroom = if elem_align > 16 then elem_align - 16 else 0 end
    let slots_request = slots_bytes + headroom
    let slots_alloc = global_alloc(slots_request, 16)
    let free_next = global_alloc(meta_bytes, 16)
    let live = global_alloc(meta_bytes, 16)
    if (slots_request != 0 && slots_alloc.is_null()) || free_next.is_null() || live.is_null() then
      panic("SlabPool::new: allocation failed - out of memory")
    end
    # Align the slot base up to the element alignment (fixed per pool —
    # the release uses the raw base below).
    mut slots = slots_alloc
    if slots_request != 0 then
      let mis = (slots_alloc as Int) & ((elem_align as Int) - 1)
      if mis != 0 then
        slots = (slots_alloc as Int + ((elem_align as Int) - mis)) as Ptr[u8]
      end
    end

    # Wire the free list (next[i] = i + 1, the last slot links to the
    # END sentinel) and clear every occupancy flag.
    mut i: UInt = 0
    while i < capacity do
      let next_ptr = (free_next as Int + (i as Int) * (word as Int)) as Ptr[UInt]
      next_ptr.write(i + 1)
      let live_ptr = (live as Int + (i as Int) * (word as Int)) as Ptr[UInt]
      live_ptr.write(0)
      i = i + 1
    end

    SlabPool {
      _slots: slots,
      _slots_alloc: slots_alloc,
      _stride: elem_stride,
      _slots_bytes: slots_request,
      _free_next: free_next,
      _live: live,
      _meta_bytes: meta_bytes,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _free_head: 0,
      _len: 0
    }
  end

  def capacity(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity
  end

  # Live occupant count (occupied slots).
  def len(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._len
  end

  def is_empty(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len == 0
  end

  def is_full(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len >= self._capacity
  end

  # Whether the slot currently holds a live occupant.
  def occupied(self: Self, index: UInt) -> Bool
    if index >= self._capacity then
      return false
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    live_ptr.read() != 0
  end

  # Ownership-taking acquire: consumes the item into a free slot and
  # returns the slot index. A full pool (no free slot) does NOT consume
  # the item — it is returned to the caller as Err(item) (the
  # sink-rejected-value full policy of std::fixed). Allocation-free.
  def acquire(inout self: Self, sink item: T) -> Result[UInt, T]
    if self._free_head >= self._capacity then
      return Result::Err(item)
    end
    let idx = self._free_head
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let next_ptr = (self._free_next as Int + (idx as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    self._free_head = next_ptr.read()
    # The item lands in the slot BEFORE the occupancy flag is set (the
    # flag is the drop authority; nothing in between can panic).
    let slot = (self._slots as Int + (idx as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    slot.write(item)
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (idx as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    live_ptr.write(1)
    self._len = self._len + 1
    Result::Ok(idx)
  end

  # Ownership-transferring release: moves the live occupant of `index`
  # out (the caller owns the returned value), clears the flag and
  # pushes the slot onto the free-list head (LIFO: the next acquire
  # reuses this slot — deterministic). None for an out-of-range or
  # already-vacant index (a double release is a no-op). Allocation-free.
  def release(inout self: Self, index: UInt) -> Option[T]
    if index >= self._capacity then
      return Option::None
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    if live_ptr.read() == 0 then
      return Option::None
    end
    let slot = (self._slots as Int + (index as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    let value = slot.read()
    live_ptr.write(0)
    let next_ptr = (self._free_next as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    next_ptr.write(self._free_head)
    self._free_head = index
    self._len = self._len - 1
    Option::Some(value)
  end

  # Deterministic teardown of the live occupants: a single ascending
  # scan of the slots; every live occupant is released (its drop runs
  # as the returned Option is discarded — the std::fixed ArrayVec::clear
  # / std::collections VecDeque::clear drop convention) and every slot
  # returns to the free list. The pool is fully reusable afterwards
  # (len 0; acquires reuse the freed slots in LIFO order). Note: this
  # drops the occupant VALUES — an occupant that owns resources (a
  # String) releases its own memory through its own drop; the pool's
  # metadata itself is untouched by any allocator.
  def clear(inout self: Self) -> Unit
    mut i: UInt = 0
    while i < self._capacity do
      self.release(i)
      i = i + 1
    end
  end
end

# By-value projected read of an occupant (Copy only — sound
# duplication: the slot keeps its value; teardown still drops it).
impl[T: Copy] SlabPool[T]
  def get(self: Self, index: UInt) -> Option[T]
    if index >= self._capacity then
      return Option::None
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    if live_ptr.read() == 0 then
      return Option::None
    end
    let slot = (self._slots as Int + (index as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    let value = slot.read()
    Option::Some(value)
  end
end

impl[T] Drop for SlabPool[T]
  def drop(inout self: SlabPool[T]) -> Unit
    # Teardown: destruct every live occupant EXACTLY ONCE — the same
    # deterministic ascending scan as clear(), over the occupancy
    # flags (the drop authority) — then release the three init-time
    # allocations (the slot arena by its RAW base and exact padded
    # request; the metadata buffers by their exact sizes) and null
    # every field. Field nulling keeps an early/manual release
    # idempotent; the double-free defense is the type engine's
    # non-Copy classification (see the module header's DROP
    # DISCIPLINE note).
    if !self._slots.is_null() && self._capacity != 0 then
      let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
      mut i: UInt = 0
      while i < self._capacity do
        let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (i as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
        if live_ptr.read() != 0 then
          let slot = (self._slots as Int + (i as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
          slot.drop_in_place()
        end
        i = i + 1
      end
      global_dealloc(self._free_next, self._meta_bytes, 16)
      global_dealloc(self._live, self._meta_bytes, 16)
      global_dealloc(self._slots_alloc, self._slots_bytes, 16)
    end
    self._slots = Ptr::null()
    self._slots_alloc = Ptr::null()
    self._free_next = Ptr::null()
    self._live = Ptr::null()
    self._capacity = 0
    self._free_head = 0
    self._len = 0
    self._slots_bytes = 0
    self._meta_bytes = 0
  end
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ArenaVec[T] — the arena-backed fixed builder (the allocator-generic
# collections row of the audit)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The audit's minimum for the finance hot path is "Vec[T, A:
# Allocator]/Map[K, V, H, A] OR an explicit builder so the hot path
# establishes STATICALLY: general-heap calls = 0 after initialization".
# A general allocator parameter on the growable containers is NOT
# expressible in kernel std today (Vec is a compiler-registered shape
# whose growth lowers to the __intrinsic_array_push intrinsic — the
# runtime allocator — see FUTURE WORK below), so the kernel-expressible
# alternative is the EXPLICIT BUILDER, implemented here.
#
# ArenaVec[T] is a Vec-like, fixed-capacity builder that OWNS a
# BumpArena: construction performs the builder's ONE allocator call
# (the arena backing); push bumps an element out of the arena, get
# reads a slot by value, len/capacity/is_full are field reads, reset is
# two stores — the general heap is never touched again (pinned by the
# unit suite's outstanding-bytes test). Elements must be Copy
# (`impl[T: Copy]` gates the whole surface): the arena reclaims raw
# bytes and never destructs occupants, so owning elements have no home
# here — their pool is SlabPool[T], which destructs them exactly once.
# The capacity is fixed at construction; push on a full builder returns
# the item unconsumed (Result::Err(item) — the sink-rejected-value
# full policy of std::fixed/SlabPool).
#
# Ownership: ArenaVec declares NO registered Drop — the BumpArena field
# is the owner and the engine's field-drop glue releases it exactly
# once at ArenaVec's drop (the Slice-embeds-ArcStrong pattern of
# std::collections). Because the field is a registered-Drop type, the
# type-property engine classifies ArenaVec[T] non-Copy structurally
# (same axis as BumpArena/SlabPool) — a bit-copy is a compile error.
# reset() clears the builder logically (len 0 + arena bump reset); the
# memory is reclaimed only at Drop. NOT thread-safe.

struct ArenaVec[T]
  _arena: BumpArena   # the owned backing arena (new() is the ONLY allocator call)
  _stride: UInt       # element stride: size_of[T] rounded up to align_of[T]
  _capacity: UInt     # element capacity (fixed at construction)
  _len: UInt          # live element count (elements [0, _len) are occupied)
end

impl[T: Copy] ArenaVec[T]
  # Creates a builder for `capacity` elements. The element alignment
  # must be a power of two <= the module anchor (policy_max_align());
  # the backing arena is pre-aligned to the element alignment when it
  # exceeds 16, so every element lands aligned. Zero capacity creates
  # an empty builder that rejects every push.
  def new(capacity: UInt) -> ArenaVec[T]
    let elem_size = size_of[T]()
    if elem_size == 0 then
      panic("ArenaVec::new: zero-size element types are not supported")
    end
    let elem_align = align_of[T]()
    if elem_align == 0 || (elem_align & (elem_align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("ArenaVec::new: element alignment must be a power of two")
    end
    if elem_align > policy_max_align() then
      panic("ArenaVec::new: element alignment " + elem_align.to_string() + " > " + policy_max_align().to_string() + " not supported - elements are aligned through the 64-byte anchor (the widest vector alignment)")
    end
    # Rounded element stride: size % align is not ABI-guaranteed, so
    # the stride is size_of[T] rounded up — element i lives at
    # base + i * stride and every element is aligned; the bump
    # advances by exactly stride per push (no padding).
    let stride_mask = elem_align - 1
    let stride = (elem_size + stride_mask) & ~stride_mask
    if stride < elem_size then
      panic("ArenaVec::new: element stride overflow")
    end
    let bytes = stride * capacity
    if capacity != 0 && bytes / capacity != stride then
      panic("ArenaVec::new: backing overflow")
    end
    # Element aligns <= 16 ride the arena's 16-aligned base with zero
    # headroom (BumpArena::new); 32/64-aligned elements need the
    # pre-aligned base (BumpArena::new_aligned, which pads the request
    # by the alignment headroom).
    let backing = if elem_align <= 16 then
      BumpArena::new(bytes)
    else
      BumpArena::new_aligned(bytes, elem_align)
    end
    ArenaVec {
      _arena: backing,
      _stride: stride,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _len: 0
    }
  end

  def capacity(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity
  end

  def len(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._len
  end

  def is_empty(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len == 0
  end

  def is_full(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len >= self._capacity
  end

  # Ownership-taking push (Vec::push shape): consumes the item into
  # element [_len] of the arena. A full builder does NOT consume the
  # item — it is returned to the caller as Err(item). The general heap
  # is not involved: push bumps the arena (deterministic; the arena's
  # exact capacity of capacity * stride bytes serves exactly
  # `capacity` pushes).
  def push(inout self: Self, sink item: T) -> Result[Unit, T]
    if self._len >= self._capacity then
      return Result::Err(item)
    end
    let slot = self._arena.alloc(self._stride, align_of[T]())
    if slot.is_null() then
      panic("ArenaVec::push: backing exhausted - the builder's capacity accounting is broken")
    end
    let element = (slot as Int) as Ptr[T]
    element.write(item)
    self._len = self._len + 1
    Result::Ok(())
  end

  # By-value projected read of element `index` (Copy only — sound
  # duplication: the element keeps its value). None for an
  # out-of-range index. Allocation-free.
  def get(self: Self, index: UInt) -> Option[T]
    if index >= self._len then
      return Option::None
    end
    let base = self._arena.buffer()
    let element = (base as Int + (index as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    Option::Some(element.read())
  end

  # Logical free-all (the arena's zero-overhead reset): ONE bump store
  # plus the len store. The pushed elements become dead (len 0) — the
  # bytes are not touched (no memset), so a reset-then-repush cycle
  # writes the same element addresses. Elements are Copy: nothing to
  # destruct.
  def reset(inout self: Self) -> Unit
    self._arena.reset()
    self._len = 0
  end
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Usage notes
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   use std::alloc_policy
#
#   # Per-batch bump arena (the market-data-packet policy): the packet
#   # decoder bumps its records out of one arena and free-alls at the
#   # packet boundary — no per-record allocator traffic. Records whose
#   # layout needs 32/64-byte alignment (SIMD batches) fund an aligned
#   # arena once:
#   var batch = std::alloc_policy::BumpArena::new(4096)
#   let hdr = batch.alloc(64, 8)
#   let body = batch.alloc(256, 16)
#   var simd_batch = std::alloc_policy::BumpArena::new_aligned(65536, 64)
#   match std::alloc::layout_new(4096, 64)
#   when Result::Ok(l) then let lanes = simd_batch.alloc_layout(l)
#   when Result::Err(_) then ()
#   end
#   # ... fill the packet from hdr/body/lanes ...
#   batch.reset()        # one store reclaims the whole batch
#   simd_batch.reset()
#
#   # The per-symbol slab (orders/state records; preallocated once):
#   var pool = std::alloc_policy::SlabPool[Int].new(1024)
#   match pool.acquire(42)
#   when Result::Ok(slot) then
#     # ... use the opaque slot index as the record's handle ...
#     let _ = pool.release(slot)   # or release(slot) and own the value
#   when Result::Err(item) then ()  # pool full: the item returns
#   end
#
#   # The no-allocator matching loop (finance): a fixed-capacity ring
#   # funded from a preallocated slab — the loop body allocates NOTHING
#   # (policy: None/Fixed; the fill happens outside the match cycle):
#   var fund = std::alloc_policy::SlabPool[Int].new(8)
#   var ring = std::fixed::RingBuffer8[Int].new()
#   match fund.acquire(100)   # one prefill per ring slot
#   when Result::Ok(slot) then
#     let _ = ring.push(slot)
#   when Result::Err(_) then ()
#   end
#   # ... the matching loop pops ring / releases fund indices only ...
#
#   # The arena-backed builder (the finance hot path's static
#   # no-heap-after-init row): the order book's bounded staging is
#   # filled ONCE from the arena and the matching cycle only pushes/
#   # gets/resets — zero general-heap calls after construction:
#   var book = std::alloc_policy::ArenaVec[Int].new(4096)
#   match book.push(100)     # the pre-init fill happens OUTSIDE the cycle
#   when Result::Ok(_) then ()
#   when Result::Err(item) then ()   # book full: the item returns
#   end
#   match book.get(0)
#   when Option::Some(v) then ()
#   when Option::None then ()
#   end
#   book.reset()   # free-all for the next cycle (one bump store)
#
#   # Embedded/static (policy: None): the arena borrows a static buffer
#   # and the containers are std::fixed's inline forms — no heap at all.
#
# FUTURE WORK (documented gaps, kept OUT of this module's contract):
#   - A general allocator parameter on the growable containers
#     (Vec[T, A], Map[K, V, H, A]) needs kernel work: Vec growth lowers
#     to the __intrinsic_array_push intrinsic (the runtime allocator),
#     so an allocator-parameterized Vec is not expressible from std
#     today (generic bounds ARE expressible — Map[K: Hash + Eq, V],
#     impl[T: Copy], the const-generic ArrayVec of std::embedded — but
#     the REGISTERED container shape itself is fixed-arity). The
#     kernel-expressible row of the audit is ArenaVec[T], the explicit
#     arena-backed builder above; bounded hot paths choose ArenaVec /
#     SlabPool / BumpArena instead of the growable containers.
#   - Const-generic capacity (ArrayVecN-style forms spelled
#     ArrayVec[T, N]) is likewise kernel type-system work — see the
#     std::fixed header's compile-time-capacity decision.
